(begin-tx "EL-6 - the convention the internal predicates depend on, made explicit")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== EL-6 - the convention the internal predicates depend on, made explicit · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== 
(print "--- [EL-6 - the convention the internal predicates depend on, made explicit · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(print "--- [EL-6 . 01 . the client's non-empty guard is load-bearing] ---")
;;Sweeping the AQP/VCT side (for the empty-list defect family recorded in
;;Kursan/_verify_finding_EMPTY-LIST_01_index_iterating_cumulators.repl) turned up THREE more predicates that fault on an empty list --
;;  AQP-POOL::URC_OrtoUnstakeNoncesSufficient
;;  AQP-VCT::URC_VacateOrtoNoncesSufficient
;;  AQP-VCT::URC_VacateOrtoLegBeneficiaryOk
;;-- and none of them is a reachable defect, because the CLIENT rejects an empty nonce list before
;;any of them runs. They are not filed as bugs. What they establish is the codebase's actual
;;convention: THE CLIENT CHECKS NON-EMPTY; THE INTERNAL PREDICATES ASSUME IT.
;;
;;That convention is coherent and it is also unwritten and unenforced, which is the risk worth a
;;test. The assertion below pins the client-side guard those three predicates silently rely on.
;;If someone relaxes it -- widens the check, moves it, or adds a second entry path that forgets it
;;-- this fails HERE, at the cheap, readable place, instead of surfacing later as an array-bounds
;;fault from deep inside a sufficiency predicate with no indication of what was actually wrong.
;;
;;Note the wording being pinned: "positive length" is an explicit non-empty requirement, not just a
;;length-equality check. That distinction is the whole guarantee.
(map print
    [
        (expect-failure "<<EL-6>> the unstake client requires a POSITIVE-length nonce list"
            "Invalid nonces / nonce-amounts: equal positive length required"
            (TS02-C3.AQP-POOL|CC_UnstakeOrtoFungible KST.ANHD
                (U|DALOS.UDC_Makeid "ResumeVacPool") KST.ANHD KST.ANHD "MVST-98c486052a51" []))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;

(begin-tx "EL-6b - and the predicate it shields, asserted as the thing being shielded")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== EL-6b - and the predicate it shields, asserted as the thing being shielded · 01 · namespace ==== 
(print "--- [EL-6b - and the predicate it shields, asserted as the thing being shielded · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [EL-6b . 01 . the unguarded predicate underneath] ---")
;;The other half of the pair. This is what the client guard above is standing in front of. It is
;;pinned so the dependency is visible from both ends: remove the guard and THIS is what callers get.
;;When the predicates are made empty-safe, this assertion flips to a success returning true (no
;;nonces requested is vacuously sufficient) -- which is what AQP-VCT::URC_VacateOrtoLegsOk already
;;returns for an empty leg list, so the module is not even self-consistent today.
(map print
    [
        (expect-failure "<<EL-6b>> the sufficiency predicate itself faults on an empty nonce list"
            "Array index out of bounds. Length (0), Index (0)"
            (AQP-POOL.URC_OrtoUnstakeNoncesSufficient
                (U|DALOS.UDC_Makeid "ResumeVacPool") "MVST-98c486052a51" KST.ANHD KST.ANHD [] []))
        ;;the self-inconsistency, in one line: a sibling predicate handles empty fine
        (expect "<<EL-6b>> ...while a SIBLING predicate answers empty legs vacuously, without faulting"
            true
            (AQP-VCT.URC_VacateOrtoLegsOk
                (U|DALOS.UDC_Makeid "ResumeVacPool") "MVST-98c486052a51" [] [] [] []))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

